---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { Content as AuthorText } from '../author.md';
import { author, SITE_NAME } from '../lib/site';
---
<Base
  title="About and method"
  description="How Cocaine Hippos: After Us separates real science from speculation, and how the project grows with contributions."
>
  <section class="wrap prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>About and method</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      {SITE_NAME} is a speculative evolution project about what <em>invasive species</em> could become if they are
      left alone for geological time. It starts with Colombia's hippos.
    </p>

    <h2>Why invasive species?</h2>
    <p>
      Most speculative evolution takes place on invented planets or after a made-up catastrophe. Invasive species are
      a real experiment that is already running. Animals have been moved by people to places where they never
      evolved, and their new neighbours are already reacting. This makes a good starting point for thinking about
      evolution. It does not mean the project thinks invasions are good or should be left alone.
    </p>

    <h2>Three levels of certainty</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="badge conf">established</span> Species and facts that exist today, with sources.</li>
      <li><span class="badge conf">plausible</span> Future species built on close real-world parallels, such as dwarf hippos on islands.</li>
      <li><span class="badge conf">speculative</span> Bolder steps, still reasoned, but with large uncertainty.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Canon and community</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="badge canon">Canon</span> species are written by the editors and form the backbone of the scenario.
      <span class="badge community">Community</span> species are accepted submissions from visitors. Both appear in
      the tree and on the era pages, and community species always carry their creator's name.
    </p>

    <h2>Coming scenarios</h2>
    <p>The hippos are the first. Possible next ones: feral camels in Australia, cane toads, wild horses and donkeys in North America, rabbits in Australia. <a href="/contribute/?context=new-scenario">Suggest a scenario</a>.</p>

    {author.show && (
      <>
        <h2>Who's behind this</h2>
        <div class="card author">
          <Image class="photo" src={author.photo} alt={`Portrait of ${author.name}`} width={120} height={120} densities={[1, 2]} />
          <p class="who">
            {author.link ? <a href={author.link}>{author.name}</a> : author.name}{author.role && <span class="role">, {author.role.toLowerCase()}</span>}
          </p>
          <AuthorText />
        </div>
      </>
    )}

    <h2>Inspiration</h2>
    <p>
      Dougal Dixon's <em>After Man</em> (1981), <em>The Future Is Wild</em>, <em>Serina</em>, and collaborative
      projects such as Sagan&nbsp;4 and OurFoodChain. The real science comes from the researchers cited on each page.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .author { overflow: hidden; }
  .author :global(.photo) { float: left; width: 120px; height: 120px; object-fit: cover; border-radius: 50%; margin: 0 1.1rem 0.5rem 0; border: 3px solid var(--mud-soft); }
  @media (max-width: 30rem) { .author :global(.photo) { float: none; display: block; margin: 0 0 0.75rem; } }
  .author .who { font-family: var(--font-head); font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 600; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
  .author .role { font-style: italic; font-weight: 500; color: var(--muted); }
  .author :global(p:last-child) { margin-bottom: 0; }
</style>
